@node	0.4, 0.5, 0.3, Chapter 0
@section Data

This section covers tuples, lists, the language's sum type, pattern
matching, and exhaustiveness. The data abstractions of @ref{2.1} onward
stand on these forms, and the interpreted data of chapter 4's evaluator is
one discriminated union of exactly the kind built here.

@heading Tuples and records

A fixed-arity pair of values is a @newterm{tuple}: a readonly array whose
length and element types are fixed. Where the parts have meaning, a
record with named fields replaces it; that is the lesson of @ref{2.1} read
in TypeScript.

@example typescript
type Point = readonly [number, number];

const p: Point = [3, 4];
const [x, y] = p;

x
// => 3
@end example

@example typescript
interface Frac @{
  readonly num: number;
  readonly den: number;
@}

const f: Frac = @{ num: 1, den: 2 @};

f
// => @{ num: 1, den: 2 @}

const @{ num, den @} = f;

num
// => 1
@end example

@noindent
A @code{readonly} field can never be reassigned through the record, so a
record is a value the program can hand around without copying, and the
destructuring pulls the parts apart again.

@heading Sums: discriminated unions and exhaustive switch

A @newterm{discriminated union} is the sum: a closed set of variants,
every variant carrying the same shape of tag. The @code{switch} that
dispatches on the tag must cover every variant, and the return type is
what buys the guarantee:

@example typescript
type Shape =
  | @{ readonly _tag: "Circle"; readonly r: number @}
  | @{ readonly _tag: "Rect"; readonly w: number; readonly h: number @};

const area = (s: Shape): number => @{
  switch (s._tag) @{
    case "Circle":
      return Math.PI * s.r * s.r;
    case "Rect":
      return s.w * s.h;
  @}
@};

area(@{ _tag: "Circle", r: 1 @})
// => 3.141592653589793

area(@{ _tag: "Rect", w: 2, h: 3 @})
// => 6
@end example

@noindent
The tag both discriminates the variant and gives the case its fields,
which is this book's pattern matching. The guarantee is the point, and
exercise 0.4 turns it into a compile experiment: delete a case, and the
file stops compiling with the missing return named. Data-directed
dispatch, where the set of types must stay open at runtime, is a
different mechanism for a different job; @ref{2.4} builds it as a
registry for exactly that reason.

@heading Option: absence as a value

The book's computations sometimes find nothing: @code{car} of an empty
list, a lookup that misses. This edition's answer is a two-variant union
of its own, @code{Option}, so absence is a value the caller switches on,
never @code{false} and never a @code{null} smuggled through:

@example typescript
type Option<A> =
  | @{ readonly _tag: "Some"; readonly value: A @}
  | @{ readonly _tag: "None" @};

const some = <A>(value: A): Option<A> => (@{ _tag: "Some", value @});
const none: Option<never> = @{ _tag: "None" @};
@end example

@heading The cons list

The book's pairs of @ref{2.2.1} get the same treatment: a @code{List<A>}
is a union of the empty list and one cons cell, and @code{cons},
@code{car}, and @code{cdr} are the three operations on it:

@example typescript
interface Nil @{
  readonly _tag: "Nil";
@}

interface Cons<A> @{
  readonly _tag: "Cons";
  readonly head: A;
  readonly tail: List<A>;
@}

type List<A> = Nil | Cons<A>;

const nil: List<never> = @{ _tag: "Nil" @};

const cons = <A>(head: A, tail: List<A>): List<A> => (@{
  _tag: "Cons",
  head,
  tail,
@});
@end example

@example typescript
const car = <A>(l: List<A>): Option<A> =>
  l._tag === "Cons" ? some(l.head) : none;

const cdr = <A>(l: List<A>): Option<List<A>> =>
  l._tag === "Cons" ? some(l.tail) : none;

car(cons(1, nil))
// => @{ _tag: "Some", value: 1 @}

car(nil)
// => @{ _tag: "None" @}
@end example

@noindent
A @code{list} helper builds the right-nested structure from a sequence in
left-to-right order, and this edition's listings use it wherever a
display list is needed.

@heading The two equalities

TypeScript separates the two equalities the book needs, though not with
two operators. @code{===} compares the primitives by value and every
object by identity: two records with equal fields are two different
objects, so structural equality is written out, per type, as a small
function.

@example typescript
const equalFrac = (a: Frac, b: Frac): boolean =>
  a.num === b.num && a.den === b.den;

const g = @{ num: 1, den: 2 @};
const h = @{ num: 1, den: 2 @};

equalFrac(g, h)
// => true

g === h
// => false
@end example

@noindent
The written-out structural comparison asks about shape and contents;
@code{===} asks about identity. For lists the structural
comparison recurs down the pairs:

@example typescript
const equalList = <A>(
  a: List<A>,
  b: List<A>,
  equal: (x: A, y: A) => boolean,
): boolean => @{
  if (a._tag === "Nil" || b._tag === "Nil") @{
    return a._tag === b._tag;
  @}
  return equal(a.head, b.head) && equalList(a.tail, b.tail, equal);
@};
@end example

